1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,062
Emma: We've got to choose a topic for our experiment, haven't we, Jack?

2
00:00:06,062 --> 00:00:09,700
Were you thinking of something to do with seeds?

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:14,520
Jack: That's right. I thought we could look at seed germination

4
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,400
how a seed begins to grow.

5
00:00:17,400 --> 00:00:24,600
Emma: OK. Any particular reason? I know you're hoping to work in plant science eventually...

6
00:00:24,600 --> 00:00:29,874
Jack: Yeah, but practically everything we do is going to feed into that.

7
00:00:29,874 --> 00:00:33,080
No, there's an optional module on seed structure

8
00:00:33,080 --> 00:00:37,175
and function in the third year that I might do,

9
00:00:37,175 --> 00:00:39,762
so I thought it might be useful for that.

10
00:00:39,802 --> 00:00:46,100
If I choose that option, I don't have to do a dissertation module

11
00:00:46,100 --> 00:00:47,900
Emma: Good idea.

12
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:54,805
Jack: Well, I thought for this experiment we could look at the relationship between seed size

13
00:00:54,805 --> 00:00:57,826
and the way the seeds are planted.

14
00:00:57,826 --> 00:01:04,300
So, we could plant different sized seeds in different ways, and see which grow best.

15
00:01:04,300 --> 00:01:09,300
Emma: OK. We'd need to allow time for the seeds to come up.

16
00:01:09,300 --> 00:01:11,940
Jack: That should be fine if we start now

17
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:17,300
A lot of the other possible experiments need quite a bit longer.

18
00:01:17,300 --> 00:01:21,428
Emma: So that'd make it a good one to choose.

19
00:01:21,428 --> 00:01:23,580
And I don't suppose it'd need much equipment

20
00:01:24,060 --> 00:01:27,621
we're not doing chemical analysis or anything.

21
00:01:27,621 --> 00:01:33,400
Though that's not really an issue, we've got plenty of equipment in the laboratory.

22
00:01:33,400 --> 00:01:36,620
Jack: Yeah. We need to have a word with the tutor

23
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:40,600
if we're going to go ahead with it though.

24
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:42,400
I'm sure our aim's OK.

25
00:01:42,400 --> 00:01:49,200
It's not very ambitious but the assignment's only ten percent of our final mark, isn't it?

26
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:54,300
But we need to be sure we're the only ones doing it.

27
00:01:54,300 --> 00:01:57,540
Emma: Yeah, it's only five percent actually

28
00:01:57,540 --> 00:02:02,200
but it'd be a bit boring if everyone was doing it.

29
00:02:02,200 --> 00:02:07,200
Jack: Did you read that book on seed germination on our reading list?

30
00:02:07,200 --> 00:02:12,462
Emma: The one by Graves? I looked through it for my last experiment,

31
00:02:12,462 --> 00:02:18,129
though it wasn't all that relevant there. It would be for this experiment, though.

32
00:02:18,129 --> 00:02:24,200
I found it quite hard to follow - lots about the theory, which I hadn't expected.

33
00:02:24,200 --> 00:02:28,871
Jack: Yes, I'd been hoping for something more practical.

34
00:02:28,871 --> 00:02:35,100
It does include references to the recent findings on genetically-modified seeds, though.

35
00:02:35,100 --> 00:02:37,900
Emma: Yes, that was interesting.

36
00:02:37,900 --> 00:02:42,600
Jack: I read an article about seed germination by Lee Hall.

37
00:02:42,600 --> 00:02:49,100
Emma: About seeds that lie in the ground for ages and only germinate after a fire?

38
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:56,148
Jack: That's the one. I knew a bit about it already, but not about this research.

39
00:02:56,148 --> 00:03:00,552
His analysis of figures comparing the times of the fires

40
00:03:00,552 --> 00:03:07,600
and the proportion of seeds that germinated was done in a lot of detail - very impressive.

41
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:15,700
Emma: Was that the article with the illustrations of early stages of plant development? They were very clear.

42
00:03:15,700 --> 00:03:20,000
Jack: I think those diagrams were in another article.

43
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:24,314
Emma: Anyway, shall we have a look at the procedure for our experiment?

44
00:03:24,314 --> 00:03:27,300
We'll need to get going with it quite soon.

45
00:03:27,300 --> 00:03:32,500
Jack: Right. So the first thing we have to do is find our seeds

46
00:03:33,200 --> 00:03:36,929
I think vegetable seeds would be best.

47
00:03:36,929 --> 00:03:39,859
And obviously they mustn't all be the same size.

48
00:03:40,621 --> 00:03:46,100
So, how many sorts do we need? About four different ones

49
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:47,780
Emma: I think that would be enough

50
00:03:48,380 --> 00:03:51,800
There'll be quite a large number of seeds for each one.

51
00:03:51,800 --> 00:03:57,447
Jack: Then, for each seed we need to find out how much it weighs,

52
00:03:57,447 --> 00:04:03,900
and also measure its dimensions, and we need to keep a careful record of all that.

53
00:04:03,900 --> 00:04:11,817
Emma: That'll be quite time-consuming. And we also need to decide how deep we're going to plant the seeds -

54
00:04:11,817 --> 00:04:16,400
right on the surface, a few millimetres down, or several centimetres.

55
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:19,200
Jack: OK. So then we get planting

56
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:25,300
Do you think we can plant several seeds together in the same plant pot?

57
00:04:25,300 --> 00:04:30,300
Emma: No. I think we need a different one for each seed.

58
00:04:30,300 --> 00:04:37,200
Jack: Right. And we'll need to label them - we can use different coloured labels.

59
00:04:37,200 --> 00:04:40,378
Then we wait for the seeds to germinate -

60
00:04:40,378 --> 00:04:45,543
I reckon that'll be about three weeks, depending on what the weather's like.

61
00:04:45,543 --> 00:04:51,900
Then we see if our plants have come up, and write down how tall they've grown.

62
00:04:51,900 --> 00:04:54,500
Emma: Then all we have to do is look at our numbers

63
00:04:54,500 --> 00:04:57,900
and see if there's any relation between them.

64
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:04,000
Jack: That's right. So...
